🔹 What is the Slow–Fast Pointer Approach?

    The slow–fast approach uses two pointers that move at different speeds through a data structure.

    Slow pointer → moves 1 step
    Fast pointer → moves 2 steps
    Used to detect patterns, cycles, or specific positions efficiently.

🔹 Why use it?

✅ Reduces time complexity
✅ Avoids extra memory (O(1) space)
✅ Elegant solution for linked lists & arrays

🔹 Common Use Cases

Detect cycle in Linked List
Find middle of Linked List
Find start of cycle
Happy Number problem
Remove nth node from end
Check palindrome in linked list

🔹 Core Idea (Intuition)

If there is a cycle:
Fast pointer will eventually catch up to slow pointer
Like runners on a circular track 🏃‍♂️🏃‍♀️
If no cycle:
Fast pointer reaches null
🔹 Example 1: Detect Cycle in Linked List
Logic

Initialize both pointers at head
Move slow by 1, fast by 2
If they meet → cycle exists


✅ Slow–Fast Approach Checklist (Very Important)

Before using this approach, ask:

✔ Problem Understanding
 Is there repeated traversal or cycle possibility?
 Can data be traversed sequentially?
✔ Pointer Setup

 Both pointers start at correct position (usually head)
 Slow → +1 step
 Fast → +2 steps
✔ Loop Safety
 Check fast != null
 Check fast.next != null
✔ Termination
 Cycle → slow == fast
 No cycle → fast reaches null

✔ Edge Cases
 Empty list
 Single node
 Two nodes
 Self-loop